<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:docs="using:Fili.Theme.MudAvalonia.Gallery.Docs"
             x:Class="Fili.Theme.MudAvalonia.Gallery.Docs.Pages.TroubleshootingPage">
  <StackPanel Classes="doc">
    <docs:DocHeader Title="Troubleshooting"
                    Summary="Most of what goes wrong with a theme goes wrong silently: nothing throws, and the app looks almost right. What to check when a control is not themed, a key does nothing or a class does more than you asked." />

    <TextBlock Classes="h5 section" Text="A control is not themed" />
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="Check the two includes. " /><Run Classes="code" Text="FiliBaseTheme.axaml" /><Run Text=" and then " /><Run Classes="code" Text="FiliTheme.axaml" /><Run Text=", in that order, in the application's " /><Run Classes="code" Text="Styles" /><Run Text=", and no " /><Run Classes="code" Text="FluentTheme" /><Run Text=" or " /><Run Classes="code" Text="SimpleTheme" /><Run Text=" beside them: the base is this package's own." />
    </TextBlock>
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="FluentTheme or SimpleTheme is still included." /><Run Text=" Before the two includes, the Fili themes win but the other theme still loads, at start-up cost, and paints wherever the Fili theme has nothing. After them, its control themes win: controls look like that theme, with Fili's class styles laid over them. Remove it, and the Avalonia.Themes package with it." />
    </TextBlock>
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="Nothing is themed in a trimmed or browser build." /><Run Text=" A " /><Run Classes="code" Text="StyleInclude" /><Run Text=" built in C# loads its file by reflection, which trimming and NativeAOT can strip, and the app then renders with no theme at all. Write the two includes in " /><Run Classes="code" Text="App.axaml" /><Run Text=", where they compile to code." />
    </TextBlock>
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="A control type newer than the theme shows nothing." /><Run Text=" The templates are forked from Avalonia 12.1.4. A control a later Avalonia 12 adds has no template here, and Avalonia has no default theme, until a theme release covers it." />
    </TextBlock>
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="Your own subclass of a control is a type of its own." /><Run Text=" A theme is found by the control's style key, its exact type, so a " /><Run Classes="code" Text="MyButton : Button" /><Run Text=" misses the Button's and shows its bare content, with no shape, colour or states, until it says it is a Button:" />
    </TextBlock>
    <docs:CodeBlock Code="protected override Type StyleKeyOverride => typeof(Button);"
                    Margin="0,0,0,16" />
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="A DataGrid shows nothing" /><Run Text=" when its package is outside 12.1.0 up to but not including 13.0.0. The theme skips its grid templates rather than apply ones that name parts that version lacks, and writes a warning to Avalonia's log; " /><Run Classes="code" Text="LogToTrace()" /><Run Text=" on the app builder shows it. Avalonia has no default theme, so the grid then has no template unless the app includes a DataGrid theme itself. Move the package into the range." />
    </TextBlock>
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="A control MudBlazor has no counterpart for" /><Run Text=" keeps the base's look, Avalonia's Simple theme in the Fili palette. The parity page lists what is drawn." />
    </TextBlock>

    <TextBlock Classes="h5 section" Text="The app does not restore or start" />
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="NU1605, a package downgrade, on restore." /><Run Text=" The theme needs Avalonia 12.1.4 or later, below 13, and the app pins an older patch. Raise every Avalonia package together, to one version." />
    </TextBlock>
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="NotSupportedException while the theme loads." /><Run Text=" A theme dictionary keyed " /><Run Classes="code" Text="x:Key=&quot;HighContrast&quot;" /><Run Text=" throws, and takes the rest of the theme down with it: Avalonia's converter knows only Light and Dark. Key it " /><Run Classes="code" Text="{}{x:Static theme:FiliThemeVariants.HighContrast}" /><Run Text="." />
    </TextBlock>

    <TextBlock Classes="h5 section" Text="Text shows as nothing in the browser" />
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run Text="Roboto covers Latin, Greek and Cyrillic, and a browser build has no system fonts to fall back to, so Arabic, Hebrew or CJK text renders as nothing. Embed a face for the script and register it with " /><Run Classes="code" Text="FontManagerOptions.FontFallbacks" /><Run Text="; the README's Fonts section has a worked example." />
    </TextBlock>

    <TextBlock Classes="h5 section" Text="A key does nothing" />
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="Check the spelling." /><Run Text=" A key nobody declares resolves to nothing and leaves the theme's value in place, with no error. Every key an app may set is in Published resource keys:" />
    </TextBlock>
    <HyperlinkButton Content="docs/published-keys.md on GitHub"
                     NavigateUri="https://github.com/FiliArrochada/Fili.Theme.MudAvalonia/blob/master/docs/published-keys.md"
                     Margin="0,0,0,16" />
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="A *Color key in a control's Resources does nothing." /><Run Text=" Each brush resolves its colour from the application's resources, so a colour is set there. To recolour one control, set the " /><Run Classes="code" Text="*Brush" /><Run Text=" key in its resources instead." />
    </TextBlock>
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="High contrast shows your dark colours." /><Run Text=" It inherits from Dark, so a key overridden in Light and Dark only falls back to the dark override. Declare the high-contrast variant too." />
    </TextBlock>
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="FiliReducedMotion, FiliFocusRing and FiliFocusRingThickness are read by each window." /><Run Text=" Set them in the application's resources or a window's; in a panel's resources inside a window they do nothing." />
    </TextBlock>
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="A value that never changes with the variant" /><Run Text=" is a StaticResource where a DynamicResource belongs: it is looked up once, in the variant the app started in. Name a theme key with " /><Run Classes="code" Text="{}{DynamicResource}" /><Run Text="." />
    </TextBlock>
    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8" Margin="0,0,0,16">
      <TextBlock Classes="body1" Text="See also:" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      <docs:DocLink Slug="theming" Content="Theming" />
      <docs:DocLink Slug="high-contrast" Content="High contrast" />
      <docs:DocLink Slug="reduced-motion" Content="Reduced motion" />
      <docs:DocLink Slug="keyboard-focus" Content="Keyboard focus" />
    </StackPanel>

    <TextBlock Classes="h5 section" Text="A class does more than you asked" />
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="The class names are MudBlazor's ordinary words" /><Run Text=", not namespaced: " /><Run Classes="code" Text="primary" /><Run Text=", " /><Run Classes="code" Text="small" /><Run Text=", " /><Run Classes="code" Text="outlined" /><Run Text=", " /><Run Classes="code" Text="error" /><Run Text=". A control that already carries one of them for your own styles picks up the theme's too, so its hover and press can be your fill and the theme's state layer at once. Grep your app for the class list in the README before adopting the theme, and let the theme own the shape and the states." />
    </TextBlock>
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="Your style beats the theme's control theme." /><Run Text=" A style outranks a control theme's setter, so a Background you set in a style replaces the colour a class gives, in every state. To recolour a control, set its colour's key, and the states the theme draws from it follow." />
    </TextBlock>
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run FontWeight="Bold" Text="A class does something only on the controls its page names." /><Run Text=" Each component page says which Avalonia control a class goes on; on another it is just a word." />
    </TextBlock>

    <TextBlock Classes="h5 section" Text="Still stuck" />
    <TextBlock Classes="body1 p">
      <Run Text="Open an issue on the repository with the Avalonia and package versions, the variant, and the smallest XAML that shows it. A security problem goes through the repository's security policy, not an issue." />
    </TextBlock>
    <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8" Margin="0,0,0,16">
      <TextBlock Classes="body1" Text="See also:" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
      <docs:DocLink Slug="installation" Content="Installation" />
      <docs:DocLink Slug="parity" Content="MudBlazor parity" />
    </StackPanel>
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
